      <abstract>&#xA;&lt;p&gt;DevOps is a new concept that has emerged in the Development and Operations  community. It calls for closer relations between developers and operations  people with a view toward improving the quality of the services we provide.&lt;/p&gt;      </abstract>
      <description>&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I&amp;#39;ll introduce the tenants of DevOps:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Simplicity&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Relationships&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Process, automation, tooling&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Continuous improvement&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I&amp;#39;ll also focus on automation and tooling and especially some of the more  interesting open source projects (and people!) that make DevOps possible:  configuration management tools, monitoring, metrics management and  visualisation.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Finally, I&amp;#39;ll cover some of the critiques, criticisms and FUD throw at DevOps  and why the concepts are more than just marketing.&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
